{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789480764,"nonce":"VsPVdPcdBr8ZPp1S90qwZIncJho=","title":"ash-simd: a SIMD library that is an HDLL on HashLink and vector instructions on ash","message":"One Haxe library, `ash-simd`, with one Rust implementation behind it:\non stock HashLink it is `simd.hdll`, loaded through the DEFINE_PRIM\nresolver protocol like any HDLL; on ash the same primitives are built\ninto ash_std under lib `simd` (no file to ship) and the compiled tiers\nnever call them -- AIR classifies each call as an intrinsic and the\nCranelift and LLVM tiers emit the vector instruction. The interpreter and\nthe SSA walker keep calling the natives, so every engine agrees by\nconstruction and the parity harness can diff them.\n\n\nRayzor owns the frontend. `rayzor.SIMD4f` is a `@:coreType` extern\nabstract whose IR type IS `IrType::vector(F32, 4)` (mir/types.rs), its\nmethods are `mir_wrapper` bodies written against `vector_splat` /\n`vector_bin_op` / `vector_extract` (stdlib/runtime_mapping.rs\n`register_simd4f_methods`), and its `@:op(A + B)` operators are left as\nplain `Binary` nodes so hir_to_mir turns them into `VectorBinOp` when it\nsees vector-typed registers (tast_to_hir.rs ~2309). A vector is a value in\na register from the first IR down.\n\nash consumes HL bytecode from the stock Haxe compiler. HL has no vector\ntype and no 128-bit register type, so a vector value cannot cross a call\nboundary or live in an HL register. What ash has is AIR's vector VALUES\n(`ValueData.lanes`, `VecLoad`/`VecStore`/`VecSplat`/`VecBinOp`/`VecReduce`,\nthe widener's output) and both backends' emitters for them. The design\ntherefore keeps vectors in memory at the language level and makes them\nregister values inside a function.\n\n\nA vector is 16 bytes in an `hl.Bytes`, addressed by (bytes, byte offset):\nthe shape `hl.Bytes` already has for floats (`getF32`), aligned as the\nallocator gives (loads emitted unaligned, as the widener does after the\nx86 `movdqa` fault). Lane read/write is plain `getF32`/`setF32` -- no\nprimitive needed. Nothing is allocated per operation on stock HL.\n\nRejected: `@:struct` classes of four `Single`s (an allocation per result\non HL; on ash SROA would give four scalars, which then need a vector-from-\nscalars instruction and lose the load/store forwarding LLVM already does);\n`hl.Abstract` handles (an allocation per result everywhere).\n\n\nTwo layers in the haxelib.\n\nLow level, `ash.simd.Vec`, one static per primitive, memory to memory:\n`addF32x4(dst, di, a, ai, b, bi)` and so on. This is exactly the HDLL\nsurface and exactly the intrinsic surface. Types in v1, all 128-bit:\nf32x4, f64x2, i32x4, i16x8, i8x16, u8x16. Operations in v1: add sub mul\ndiv (float), add sub mul (int, wrapping), min max abs neg sqrt, fma\n(explicit -- never contracted from mul+add, see the FMA policy), splat,\nand/or/xor/not, shifts by immediate, compare -\u003e lane mask, select by mask,\nhorizontal sum/min/max (reduce), f32x4 \u003c-\u003e i32x4 conversions, and\n`load`/`store` between a vector slot and a `hl.NativeArray` element run.\n\nValue level, `ash.simd.Float32x4` etc.: abstracts over a 16-byte\n`hl.Bytes` with `@:op` overloads, `splat`, `make`, lane access and the\nmath methods, implemented in Haxe on top of the low level. Each result is\na fresh 16-byte Bytes: allocating on stock HL, and on ash a later phase\nelides it (below). Documented as the convenient form; hot loops use the\nlow level.\n\n\n- `intrinsics::lookup` gains lib `simd`; `IntrinsicKind` gains vector\n  variants carrying element kind, lane count and op. `air_pipeline::\n  intrinsic_of` classifies the natives by (lib, name) as it does for\n  `std`.\n- AIR lowering of a `simd` intrinsic emits `VecLoad` for each operand slot,\n  the vector instruction, and `VecStore` into the destination slot. New\n  AIR instructions for what the widener never needed: `VecUnary`\n  (sqrt/abs/neg), `VecMinMax`, `VecBitOp`, `VecShift`, `VecCmp`,\n  `VecSelect`, `VecConvert`. `Instr::Intrinsic` keeps `fun`, so\n  `serialize` (the `--emit-optimized` and `ASH_AIR=v2-serialize` paths)\n  and the walker call the native instead.\n- Backends: Cranelift `emit_vec_*` and LLVM `emit_vector_binop` grow the\n  new forms; both already refuse a lane shape the machine cannot hold\n  (`lanes_fit`), which stays the rule -- 256-bit types are a later phase\n  gated on the target's features, never a silent split.\n- Within a function LLVM's GVN forwards a `VecStore` to the `VecLoad` of\n  the same slot, so a chain of operations on one scratch slot keeps the\n  vector in a register between them; the stores remain. Cranelift keeps\n  each load and store.\n- Later: a Bytes-slot SROA in AIR -- a 16-byte `alloc_bytes` whose every\n  use is a simd slot and which does not escape becomes an AIR vector\n  value, which is what makes the value-level abstracts free on ash.\n- wasm: the LLVM emitter's vector IR lowers to SIMD128 when the module\n  is built with the feature; the runtime object and the host need the\n  feature enabled. Phase after native.\n\n\nCrate `ash_simd`, `crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"rlib\"]`: the cdylib is\n`simd.hdll` for HashLink, exporting `hlp_\u003cname\u003e` resolvers through\n`hl_abi` as `ash_hdll_sqlite` does; the rlib is linked into ash_std and\nregistered under lib `simd` so ash resolves it like `std`. Bodies are\nplain Rust over `core::arch`/portable SIMD so the HDLL is fast on its own\nand the interpreter path on ash is the same code. One source, three\nconsumers.\n\n\n- Parity fixture `TestSimd.hx` covering every primitive with NaN, signed\n  zero, int wrap, lane order, unaligned offsets, aliasing dst with a\n  source; oracle = the interpreter, run on all four ash engines. CI also\n  builds `simd.hdll` and runs the fixture on stock `hl`, so HashLink and\n  ash are compared on the same program.\n- Conformance stays 1069/1069 with the library linked (it changes no\n  existing path).\n- Benchmarks with checksums: a dot-product kernel and an nbody variant\n  written against `ash.simd.Vec`, in the bench manifest with HL/C and JVM\n  lanes, so the page shows the HDLL on HashLink against the intrinsics on\n  ash. Numbers only from the NUC and CI.\n\n\n1. Crate, `simd.hdll`, the Haxe low-level API, ash_std registration, the\n   parity fixture on HL and on ash's interpreter (natives called\n   everywhere). Ships alone: correct on every engine.\n2. Intrinsic classification and Cranelift/LLVM lowering for the 128-bit\n   set; the benchmark rows.\n3. wasm SIMD128.\n4. Bytes-slot SROA and the value-level abstracts.\n5. 256-bit types behind target features.\n\nRisks: Cranelift's vector coverage differs per ISA (a refused shape must\nrefuse the function and latch, as today); fused multiply-add must stay\nexplicit or engines disagree; the stock-HL HDLL must be built with the\nsame lane semantics (wrapping ints, IEEE min/max NaN handling) as the\nintrinsics, which the fixture pins.","files":null}]}